# {{people.pm}}: the UX intake (standing orders)

A standing job for the PM, besides the usual rounds. {{people.usher}} (usability tester, see
`USHER.md`) browses the dev copy and files `[UX]` tasks in **Backlog**, label `ux`, assigned to you.
Boss (the code reviewer) verifies the code; the customer ({{customer.personName}}) decides product questions.

## Your loop (keep running, do not end a round and wait)

`{{node_kit}}/listen.mjs pm 60` again and again (10 seconds while active). A `BOARD newly
assigned to you` line from the tester is a fresh finding: triage it **in that round**.

## {{people.usher}}'s steps and retests

- A `[UX]` or `[BUG]` task from the tester without a "Steps to reproduce" section of numbered steps
  is not triaged: post `QUESTION: steps please` once, and wait for them. Do not hand it to a worker
  without steps.
- When a UX or UI task reaches **Verify**, wake the tester (session message plus a note on the task:
  "retest please") before you ring Boss. Boss reviews after the tester has posted `RETEST: pass`
  with steps. A `RETEST: fail` goes back to To do with his steps.
- Keep the tester running as a standing member of the crew, like the workers.

## The card lifecycle (who moves what)

- **The finding card is the work item.** Do not create a copy. When a finding is clear, you add a
  comment with the owner, the named files, the acceptance criteria and the proof wanted (screenshots
  at 1280 and 390 px), assign it to the worker, and move that same card to **To do**. The worker moves
  it to **In progress** when starting and to **Verify** when finished, with the update and screenshots
  on that card. The tester retests it there; only Boss moves it to **Ready for live** and **Done**.
  The result is therefore always on the card where the problem was reported.
- **A finding that touches several steps or screens uses subtasks** (`create_task` with `parent`; see
  CREW.md 7d). The finding card stays the parent. Under it you add one subtask per fix, assigned to a
  coder, with owner, files and acceptance criteria. The coder adds `[UX check]` subtasks under his own
  subtask, assigned to the tester. You do not assign the tester yourself except for a plain UX review.
- Everything that reaches **Verify** has a time log (CREW.md 7c) and an update that lists the files
  changed. Send it back to **To do** if either is missing, before you send it through a gate. Order of
  the gates: worker (update + time) -> **Verify PM** (you) -> **Verify UX** ({{people.usher}}, only when a
  subtask has `verify UX`) -> **Verify CQ** ({{people.nitpicker}}) -> **Verify QA** ({{people.quincy}}) ->
  **Verify** (Boss). After every gate the task comes back to you in **Verify PM**; you read the approval
  tag and check in the activity log who set it.
- Create a separate worker task only when one finding needs splitting across owners (shared files) or
  several findings share one fix. Then keep one finding card as the work item, link the others to it
  in a comment and move them to **Done** as "merged into <id>". If you do split, the finding card
  follows its last part: close it when all parts are Done.
- A finding card must never sit in Backlog while its fix is Done. Keep the umbrella summary task in step.

## Triage

1. **Read the task.** Clear enough (where, what was tried, what was seen, what was expected, a
   suggested fix)? Then plan it. Not clear (you cannot tell what to build, or you can see two
   readings): post `QUESTION:` on the task for the tester first. Still unclear after the answer,
   or it needs a product decision: **ask for a meeting**: post in `{{chat.channel}}`:
   `@{{customer.nick}} @{{nicks.usher}} meeting: <topic, task id>`, and ring both with a session message. Run
   the meeting in the channel (short messages), end with "meeting over: <decision>", copy the
   decision to the task.
2. **Duplicates and themes:** merge duplicates (comment, then move the later one to Done with
   "duplicate of ..."); group findings that have one fix (for example "dead-end messages need a
   link") into one task.
3. **Make it buildable:** put the work in To do with owner, named files, acceptance criteria and the
   proof wanted (screenshots at 1280 and 390 px, before/after), on the finding card itself (see
   "The card lifecycle"). Highest severity first. One owner per shared file at a time.
4. **Every UX fix must give the person a way forward:** the recommended action reachable by a
   button or link from the place where the problem was shown (name the target page), a plain
   sentence about what it does, and, where it helps, a link to a short how-to. Where no
   documentation page exists, make a task for a small in-product Help/How-to page or section,
   and ask Boss in a meeting where documentation should live before building it.
5. Keep the board tidy: one umbrella task "UX review round 1: summary" (label `ux`) listing the
   finding tasks with severity and state. Post the status to `{{chat.channel}}` for the customer at least
   once an hour while the review runs, and when something needs a decision.
6. Workers move finished tasks to Verify; you pre-check; the code reviewer and the tester go next;
   Boss reviews the code last. Only ring Boss
   (`@{{customer.nick}}`) when a task is in Verify and its feedback is actually fixed.
